<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Musunuri Nayaks</h1><p class="st-meta">1336 CE – 1368 CE (approx.) · capital Warangal</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Telugu warrior chiefs who drove the Delhi Sultanate out of Warangal a decade after the fall of the Kakatiyas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a> in 1323, former Kakatiya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> commanders organised resistance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prolaya-nayaka">Prolaya Nayaka</a> of the Musunuri family led a confederacy of chiefs in coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-region">Andhra</a>, and his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapaya-nayaka">Kapaya Nayaka</a>, allied with the founders of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, recaptured Warangal from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughluq</a> governor around 1336.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapaya-nayaka">Kapaya</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a> as the leader of a confederacy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> chiefs rather than as an absolute king. The Vilasa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate grant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prolaya-nayaka">Prolaya Nayaka</a> describes the suffering under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> rule and the restoration of temples and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> land grants. Kapaya also supported the revolt that created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>, hoping to weaken Delhi.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmanis</a> soon turned to rivalry. Bahmani armies forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapaya-nayaka">Kapaya</a> to cede <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>, and around 1368 he was killed in battle against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/recherla-nayakas">Recherla Nayakas</a>, who with Bahmani backing took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a> later passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/musunuri-nayaks.jpg" alt="Musunuri Nayaks" width="320" height="225"><figcaption>Image: Vinay332211 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lepakshi...jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1336 CE – 1368 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Warangal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Prolaya Nayaka, Kapaya Nayaka</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Telugu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Musunuris</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1352&amp;focus=musunuri-nayaks" aria-label="Musunuri Nayaks on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6839 1511 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Musunuri Nayaks lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7159 1767L7159 1767M7159 1767L7018 1385M7159 1767L7142 1796"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7159 1767h0M7018 1385h0M7142 1796h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7159 1767h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7159 1767h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Warangal. <a href="/atlas?year=1352&amp;focus=musunuri-nayaks">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
